relic NEWS -- changes a user or an embedder of relic would notice, by release

relic 1.0.0 (unreleased)

  The first release: a Datalog engine for C, as a library, librelic, and a
  command-line tool, relic(1).

  * The language: facts, rules and queries over symbols, strings and 64-bit
    integers, with stratified negation, where _ in a negated atom is a
    column it does not test, so that not order(_, C, _) holds when C has
    no order at all; integer arithmetic in comparisons;
    the string functions length, cat, substr, index, match and str, match
    taking a linear-time regular expression; and the aggregates count, sum,
    min and max.  Digits followed by a period and a digit, or by an
    exponent, as in 1.5 or 1e5, are an error: there are no floating-point
    numbers, and 1.5 is reserved for them, as are # and $ outside a string
    or a comment.
  * Evaluation: semi-naive fixed points, secondary indexes built when a plan
    first asks for one, joins ordered by estimated cost, each round of a
    recursive rule choosing between two join orders by the work it
    estimates for each over what the round reads, including the work of
    building an index the order needs and its relation lacks, which is
    built only when a round first chooses that order, an atom that binds
    nothing the rest of its rule reads, through _ or a variable named once,
    tested for a matching tuple rather than joined once per match, and
    queries answered through a Magic Sets rewrite, which restricts what is
    derived to what the query's constants reach wherever negation and the
    rewrite's size allow, and otherwise evaluates in full.  A recursion
    that passes the free columns of a bound call through unchanged, as a
    transitive closure queried from a node does, is walked from each bound
    value and answered for it alone, rather than for every node the walk
    reaches.  Rounds of evaluation, and the reading of a large imported
    table on eight threads or more, can be shared among threads, with the
    same answers in the same order whatever their number.
  * Optional limits on the tuples held and on the memory relations, values,
    plans and rewrites occupy: a load, an evaluation or a query that would
    exceed one is refused with a diagnostic, and changes nothing.  A
    query's Magic rewrite is held to a limit on its work, in proportion to
    the program unless set; a rewrite that outgrows it is abandoned with a
    warning, and the query answered from the whole program.
  * The tool: answers in text, CSV, TSV or JSON; facts imported from CSV and
    TSV tables, -I edge.tsv naming its predicate and format by its file name
    and -T edge:symbol,symbol declaring the predicate's column types once;
    whole relations written to files with -O path.tsv, in CSV, TSV, JSON or
    as facts, -O path.dl, that a later run loads back as a program;
    an interactive mode with line editing where libedit is present, where
    control-C stops the query running and returns to the prompt; the plan of
    every evaluation with -e and its counters with -X stats.
  * The library: one header, <relic.h>, a pkg-config module, librelic, and
    only relic_ symbols exported.  A failed call returns -1 or NULL, and an
    operation on a context explains its failure through a diagnostic
    callback and relic_error(); no ordinary failure exits the process.
    relic_error_class() says what kind of failure it was -- input the
    language rejects, an evaluation error, a limit, a file, memory, an
    interrupt, a malformed call or a bug in relic -- so that a program
    need not read the text.
    An answer is streamed to a row callback, or collected into a result
    that holds its own copy of every symbol and string and outlives the
    context, read with relic_result_string() and ordered with
    relic_result_compare().
    relic_interrupt() stops an evaluation in progress, from a signal
    handler, another thread or a callback, and leaves the context ready
    for the next.
  * No runtime dependency beyond the C library; libedit is optional.
  * Optional ThinLTO builds, enabled with --enable-thinlto, check compiler,
    archive and linker compatibility during configuration. Object audits
    cover LLVM bitcode as well as native objects.
  * Built and tested on macOS, on Linux with glibc and with musl, and on
    FreeBSD.
